{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789902504,"nonce":"ZkbDFo4W0JIlp2M2aV4gi7pQ4hI=","title":"Lua: a library function called as a value names itself unqualified in its errors","message":"The reference names a function the way it was reached: table.insert({}, 'x', 1) raises \"bad argument #2 to 'insert'\" but pcall(table.insert, {}, 'x', 1) raises \"bad argument #2 to 'table.insert'\". The value wrappers qualify the argument-kind checks (zl_arg_int and the like take the message's start) but every check written into a library function's body carries the unqualified name: 'move', 'sort', 'fmod', 'floor'. The name would have to travel into the body, or the body raise a code the wrapper words.","files":null}]}